1
00:00:01,050 --> 00:00:07,514
Briefing for ministries of finance and utilities. Absent at signature. Present when it is paid.

2
00:00:10,614 --> 00:00:13,686
Enilec Mok: We usually see the project when its terms are nearly settled.

3
00:00:13,986 --> 00:00:19,511
Paul Ahmak: And the utility may not even be a party to the MoU. The obligations come to us later.

4
00:00:21,261 --> 00:00:31,970
Qua Iboe, Nigeria. Its sponsors were credible, yet it stalled. An official told Reuters the plant would enter a system that is illiquid and insolvent.

5
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:43,753
The national oil company said the bulk buyer appeared reluctant to commit to new projects, for fear of adding to its liabilities.

6
00:00:46,803 --> 00:00:55,230
In Ghana, the country was reported to be paying more than five hundred million dollars a year for electricity it did not use.

7
00:00:58,280 --> 00:01:11,336
And even a well-structured project, Cameroon's Nachtigal, was reported in 2025 to have largely drawn its payment guarantee. A strong project stays exposed to a weak buyer.

8
00:01:14,386 --> 00:01:18,674
Decision note. Can the state carry the backstop if the buyer cannot?

9
00:01:19,574 --> 00:01:25,291
Lenders will ask for guarantees, fixed capacity payments and hard currency tariffs.

10
00:01:25,741 --> 00:01:30,221
Finance usually sees the project when its terms are nearly settled.

11
00:01:30,671 --> 00:01:34,554
And a weak buyer exposes even well-structured projects.

12
00:01:35,004 --> 00:01:43,879
Your decision: see every MoU that could lead to fixed payments or state support before signature, not when lenders arrive with term sheets.

13
00:01:45,079 --> 00:01:52,140
Apply the state's test, and size support to fiscal space. And for the utility: can we pay for what we buy?

14
00:01:54,590 --> 00:01:55,273
Stop.

15
00:01:55,773 --> 00:01:58,418
You are the Director of Public Debt.

16
00:01:58,918 --> 00:02:02,289
An MoU was signed two years ago, without you.

17
00:02:02,689 --> 00:02:05,633
Lenders now ask for a State guarantee.

18
00:02:06,033 --> 00:02:08,657
The tariff is in U.S. dollars.

19
00:02:09,057 --> 00:02:10,123
What do you do?

20
00:02:17,323 --> 00:02:21,654
The state must read the contract as carefully as its lenders do.

21
00:02:23,704 --> 00:02:25,197
Come in at the start.

22
00:02:26,097 --> 00:02:29,169
Not when lenders arrive with their term sheets.
